# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
leave_by_place	one
removal_darkened	one
prodigal_physical	one
distance_affections	one
# Augustine's point is that "leaving", "removal", "far country" and "distance" from God are not spatial.
# Each claim keeps that non-spatial sense in every statement that uses it. The spatial sense only comes up to be denied.
# P3 reads the prodigal's "far country" figuratively, as P2 and C do. "True distance" in S7 names that sense outright, so A2 passes no word from a spatial sense to a spiritual one.
